      "side": 1,
      "message": "The reason we add \"|| defined(__PKVM_HYP__)\" is to disable exports (define DISABLE_EXPORTS). So that \"!defined(__DISABLE_EXPORTS) || defined(__PKVM_HYP__)\" can still be true.\n\nAnd the reason we disable exports because the arch/x86/lib/retpoline.S are exporting some symbols via EXPORT_SYMBOL, which doesn\u0027t have implementation in the pkvm. Alternative, rather than define DISABLE_EXPORTS here + aosp/3659737, changing include/linux/export.h by below is also fine.\ndiff --git a/include/linux/export.h b/include/linux/export.h\nindex a8c23d945634..e4ec88ce75dd 100644\n--- a/include/linux/export.h\n+++ b/include/linux/export.h\n@@ -32,7 +32,7 @@\n                __EXPORT_SYMBOL_REF(sym)        ASM_NL  \\\n        .previous\n\n-#if defined(__DISABLE_EXPORTS)\n+#if defined(__DISABLE_EXPORTS) || defined(__PKVM_HYP__)\n\nIt is much easier. But I feel like __DISABLE_EXPORTS is defined for the purpose of removing EXPORT_SYMBOL. And the files in aosp/3659737 are using __DISABLE_EXPORTS to decide if the code is kernel code or not. Probably a cleaner way is have another macro to be used to indicate a code is kernel code or not rather than reusing __DISABLE_EXPORTS. If this is true, then the current way of define DISABLE_EXPORTS here + aosp/3659737 is better.",
